    "lawCode": "Add §99-r, St Fin L; add §623, Tax L; add §18-104.1, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A2769A\n\nTITLE  OF BILL:   An act to amend the state finance law, the tax law and\nthe administrative code of the city of New York, in relation to creating\nthe New York city parks, tree and urban forestry fund\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: To establish the New York  City  parks,\ntrees  and  urban  forestry  fund  and  to provide funding by means of a\ncheckoff on city income tax returns. This fund would be used exclusively\nfor the purpose outlined in Article 53 of the Environmental Conservation\nLaws of 1978.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: This bill establishes  legislative-find-\nings and intent for funding a New York city parks, tree and urban fores-\ntry fund and it authorizes the State Comptroller and the Commissioner of\nTaxation and Finance to jointly establish this special fund. It shall be\nthe  duty  of the commissioner of the department of parks and recreation\nof New York City to ensure that such  moneys  are  spent  only  for  the\npurposes  stated.  Moneys  of  such  fund shall be made available to the\ncommissioner of the department of parks and recreation of New York city,\nupon such submission of certified vouchers and shall be  used  only  for\nthe planting of trees and shrubs, park preservation and expansion, main-\ntenance  and  improvement  of  botanical gardens in New York city and to\nfurther effectuate the stated purposes of article  53  of  the  environ-\nmental  conservation  law. The commissioner shall make available up to,-\nbut not more than, fifteen percent of the total amount in the  fund  for\nthe maintenance and improvement of botanical gardens in New York City.\n\nThis  bill  amends  the  tax law to provide for a Gift for New York City\nparks, trees and urban forestry fund to be placed on the personal income\ntax return section of the New York City income tax  form.    Individuals\nresiding in New York City may elect to contribute to this fund by having\na  refund  credited  to the fund or by indicating that an overpayment of\ntaxes owed is to be credited to the fund.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: In recent years there is an increasing awareness of world\nwide global warming and the effect of the carbon  dioxide  emissions  on\nthat  global warming. Six of the hottest years on record occurred in the\n1980's. Winters here in the Northeast have become wanner and  drier.  It\nis  common  for  television weather reporters to refer to global warming\nwhen discussing our weather. Global warming not only affects our comfort\nlevel but the cost of energy and the quality of air that we breathe.\n\nSeveral important facts  need  to  be  remembered  when  discussing  the\nsolutions to global warming.\n\n-greenhouse gas emissions, created by the burning of fossil fuels in our\ncars and power plants, linger in the atmosphere for decades.\n\n-reforestation  is  the only post-combustion measure that can contribute\nto reducing the threat of global warming.\n\n-according to a recent study by the New  York  State  Energy  Office  on\ncarbon reduction, planting of trees in urban areas is one of, if not the\nmost,  cost effective measure for reducing carbon emissions. Its cost is\na minus $9 per ton of carbon reduced.\n\nAll  these  pertinent  facts  are  addressed by this bill. For years the\nstate and city has looked into the federal government to fund reforesta-\ntion efforts. Funds in past years have dwindled to $10,000 for statewide\nefforts. We can no longer wait for other venues to fund  this  important\nand cost effective measure. We should give the people of the City of New\nYork  the  opportunity  to  act locally while thinking globally. The tax\nreturn checkoff would enable people who wish to better their  own  envi-\nronment  as  well as contribute to a worldwide reduction in carbon emis-\nsions the opportunity to do so without  using  general  fund  moneys  in\nthese tough fiscal times.\n\nPRIOR  LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: 1991-92: A.10389 referred to Tourism.  1993-\n94: A.3263 referred to Tourism. 1995: A.4671 passed Tourism, referred to\nWays and Means. 1996: A.4671 referred to Tourism. 1997:   A.5352  passed\nAssembly,  1998:  A.5352  passed Assembly, 1999: A.5407 passed Assembly,\nreferred to Senate Finance. 2000: 5407:  returned  to  Assembly,  passed\nAssembly,  referred  to  Senate  Finance.  2001:A.4448  passed Assembly.\n2002:A4448-b passed Assembly. 2003:A1427 passed Assembly. 2904: A 1427-A\npassed Assembly. 2005: A1820 referred to Tourism, Arts and Sports Devel-\nopment. 2006: A1820 passed Assembly.  2007-08: A.2164 passed Assembly.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: Because this is essentially a bill to provide fund-\ning, there would be little fiscal impact.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect January  1,  2012;  provided,\nhowever,  that  the  department of taxation and finance shall effectuate\nthe provisions of section of this act on or before such effective date.\n\n",
